-[%end-note%]
+During the life-cycle of an encounter it will pass through many statuses.  There are a few different types of status that can be tracked:
+</p>
+<ul>
+<li><b>status</b> - The overall current encounter status (e.g., in-progress)</li>
+<li><b>businessStatus</b> - A more granular business status (e.g., pre-admitted for inpatient)</li>
+<li><b>subjectStatus</b> - The status of the patient with respect to the encounter (e.g., arrived)</li>
+</ul>
+<p>
+In general terms the Encounter and Appointment both align with the <a href="https://build.fhir.org/lifecycle.html#clinical">Clinical Workflow Process Life Cycle</a> pattern.
+</p>
+<p>
+Note that the businessStatus may have different value sets based on the specific workflows. 
+For example, it may track check-in and check-out for outpatient visits, but admit and discharge for inpatient visits. 
+The workflows tracked in businessStatus.type are expected to line up with Encounter.class values in most cases.
+</p>
+<p>
+For example in a hospital emergency department the subjectStatus would reflect the patient's status e.g. arrived (when the patient first presents to the ED), triaged (when the patient is assessed by a triage nurse), etc.
+</p>
+<p>
+There is no direct indication purely by the status or subjectStatus field as to whether an encounter is considered "admitted". 
+Thus, a businessStatus.code = "admitted" with a businessStatus.type = "inpatient" can indidate that the patient is admitted in an inpatient context. 
+The context of the encounter and business practices/policies/workflows/types can influence this definition. (e.g., acute care facility, aged care center, outpatient clinic, emergency department, community-based clinic).
 </p>
